WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Managing this massive risk profile cannot
fall on a single department.

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.300
Instead, it requires specific, active
participation from distinct groups.

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.140
This diagram presents ethical integrity as
a distributed structure on three

4
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.680
pillars. On the right, administrators
handle institutional defense and

5
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.440
reputation. In the center, researchers
carry the foundational responsibility

6
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.780
of navigating daily ethical dilemmas.

7
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.300
On the left, the Research Ethics Committee
provides independent oversight

8
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.140
before work begins. The tension here is
deliberate.

9
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:38.860
If any single pillar abdicates its
specific duty, the entire architectural

10
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.020
integrity of the research collapses.

11
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.360
When those pillars fail, the abstract
cultural issues solidify into hard,

12
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.580
legal, and operational consequences.

13
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.280
Here are four specific regulatory risks
institutions must actively defend

14
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.580
against. First, direct violations of
frameworks like GDPR trigger severe

15
00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:04.040
penalties. Second, oversight failures like
skipping biosafety protocols halt

16
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.480
operations. Third, intellectual property
disputes lead to direct litigation.

17
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.800
Finally, containment failures represent
physical risks, like genetic

18
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.960
modifications escaping the lab.

19
00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.360
These categories are not theoretical.

20
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.880
They are the measurable, legally
actionable outcomes that occur when

21
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.500
underlying structural flaws are ignored.

22
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.780
Vulnerabilities mutate depending on your
discipline.

23
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.120
IT and AI risks are technical and
scalable, like algorithmic bias or

24
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.680
autonomous systems causing physical harm.

25
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.480
Conversely, social and behavioral sciences
face human-centric threats,

26
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:46.040
primarily the deception of vulnerable
subjects and devastating confidentiality

27
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.940
breaches. Blanket policies cannot cover
these diverging threat models.

28
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.092
Effective mitigation requires highly
specialized, field-specific awareness.
